<section>
    <div class="section-head">
        <div>
            <div class="section-number">02 / ADVANCED INTELLIGENCE</div>
            <h2>The Hidden Variables</h2>
        </div>
        <p class="section-intro">
            Geographic advantage rarely comes from a single number. It emerges from interactions
            between variables that are usually evaluated separately.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="insight-grid">
        <article class="insight-card">
            <div class="number">01 — DEMAND</div>
            <h3>Demand-Skill Misalignment</h3>
            <p>
                A capability can appear ordinary in a large talent pool yet become unusually valuable
                when deployed against a shortage that employers or customers cannot easily resolve.
            </p>
            <div class="reveal">
                The useful metric is not simply “how many jobs exist.” Examine whether the problems being
                purchased are growing faster than the number of people capable of solving them.
            </div>
            <button class="reveal-btn" onclick="toggleInsight(this)">Explore mechanism →</button>
        </article>

        <article class="insight-card">
            <div class="number">02 — FRICTION</div>
            <h3>Competition Is Only One Cost</h3>
            <p>
                Saturation affects more than visibility. It can increase customer acquisition effort,
                salary pressure, networking requirements, and the amount of proof required before trust is established.
            </p>
            <div class="reveal">
                A smaller market can therefore outperform a larger one if access costs, competitor density,
                and customer acquisition friction are substantially lower.
            </div>
            <button class="reveal-btn" onclick="toggleInsight(this)">Explore mechanism →</button>
        </article>

        <article class="insight-card">
            <div class="number">03 — NETWORK</div>
            <h3>Proximity Changes Opportunity Flow</h3>
            <p>
                Certain ecosystems compress the distance between talent, capital, customers,
                institutions, mentors, vendors, and decision-makers.
            </p>
            <div class="reveal">
                Network density matters because opportunity often travels through repeated interactions.
                A strong position can reduce the number of steps required to reach the people who can
                accelerate your next move.
            </div>
            <button class="reveal-btn" onclick="toggleInsight(this)">Explore mechanism →</button>
        </article>
    </div>
</section>


